#### The Gender Earnings-Gap

In 2025 in the UK, the total weekly earnings per working age man was £179. The total weekly earnings per working aged woman was £7. 

This difference could be due to:  

- a greater number of men in the "labour force" (people who are employed or actively seeking employent. it excludes students etc).  
- more employment for men in the labour force compared to women in the labour force
- men working more hours per work than women (i.e., fewer part time roles)
- a higher average hourly wage for men compared to women (this is the "gender pay gap", unadjusted for specific role)

You can find this data for a whole load of different countries at [https://ilostat.ilo.org/data/?cat_mode=subject](https://ilostat.ilo.org/data/?cat_mode=subject){target="_blank"}, if you would like to do this for somewhere else. 

```{r}
#| echo: false
df <- tribble(
  ~pop,~working_agepop,~labourforce,~employees,~avg_hours_worked,~avg_hourly_earnings,
    "males",	23718900,	18442860,	4823169,	37.9,	23.2,
  "females",	24613110,	5767405	,280557,	31.7,	20.1
) |> mutate(
  #emplrate = employees/labourforce,
  #pptrate = labourforce/working_agepop,
  total_weekly_earnings = avg_hourly_earnings*avg_hours_worked*employees, .after = employees
) |> select(-avg_hourly_earnings)

df |>  gt::gt()
```

